    "turn_text": "Right, but we often, what we say about ourselves, we define ourselves. And I'm not good at playing golf, not good. I go out there, I'm terrible. But if I put my mind to it, if you and I went out there and practiced as much as Tiger Woods did, we wouldn't be Tiger Woods, but we would be good at golf. And so what I'm just saying is, you and I did not choose to be math geniuses. That's not what we were called to this world to do. We didn't put the time in. But you are a guy that does love art and poetry and song. And you've mastered that. I tell you, I think that the most powerful force in the world is artists of activism, people that use their gifts of the art to inspire, to move, to challenge. And I think that this time right now in America, We need artists like we've never needed them before to expand the moral imagination of this country. And so you and I are here because of, you know, Harry Belafonte, I could go through the artist, Paul Robeson, you know, these great artists who lived around Tom Chow, Langston Hughes, you know, we, I just was reading a lot. A friend of mine wrote a book about James Baldwin, this incredible artist, Maya Angelou, I mean, We are here because artists spoke to the spirit and the heart and the harm and the scars of black people, the blues, jazz, rap, all of these",
