  {
    "episode_id": 77710,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77710.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "So look, I'm driving through Newark, New Jersey right now, man. I moved to this community more than two decades ago now, back in the 90s. And I came here as a young lawyer just coming out of Yale Law School. And, you know, look, I joke with you, my dad really, mom really raised me that, You are your success is judged by the contribution you make to others. So I came here to represent people that couldn't afford a lawyer. And so I started getting involved representing tenants against some lords and got to know the tenant leaders really well. And they challenged me. They stepped to me pretty hard and just said, hey, we want you to run for city council. And it wasn't my plan. And it was an impossible race because I was the youngest person ever to try to. And when I eventually won that race, I was the youngest person ever elected in Newark's municipal history. So it wasn't my plan. I did it because the people who I was trying to serve really talked to me about that's the best The best way you could help us is to get involved in helping to change the politics of the town. I just pulled up in front of my house. It was right up the street from where I am right now where the projects that I would move into. We took on the slum lord. He eventually got convicted in federal court. We won some big battles and it launched me into city politics that I eventually became the mayor of Newark.",
