  {
    "episode_id": 77710,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77710.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Look, I'm only the fourth black person ever elected in the nation's history to popularly elected the United States Senate. So before me, Barack Obama, before him, a woman named Carol Mosley Braun, before him, before him, a man named Edmund Brooke back in the 70s. And, you know, you walk onto that floor, you know, this is where they debated, you know, in that building slavery, this is where they debated the civil rights movement. This is where people like Strom Thurmond spill a buster, the Civil Rights Act for the longest filibuster in history. So you walk on that, you have to know the only reason you're there. were because of the incredible sacrifices and struggles of those who came before. So look, I get frustrated at times. I get it was hard to, as you said, being going from an executive where you're the mayor and now you're part of a 100 person body. It was a transition, but I feel so blessed and so grateful that every day I get to leave Newark, New Jersey, or once a week, I go down there, you'll Mondays, you should come back on Thursdays, and I get to fight for the issues that I was fighting for here in the city. Criminal justice reform, environmental justice, too much from letting our kids water to kids with asthma. I get to fight with economic opportunity. Why do people in my neighborhood work full-time jobs, catch extra shifts, but still have to use food stamps at times at the Bodega up the street. These are the things I get to fight for now for New Jersey and our nation and actually pass laws. I got a law passed last year that liberated thousands of people from jail. And so it gives you the sense every day that you've got a mission and if you get something accomplished, it means a real difference in people's lives.",
