    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77710.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "I mean, yeah, I mean, when you say experience racism, let's be clear. I'm sitting in the neighborhood where there's, you experience structural racism right here next to me, right? I'm sitting right outside of the integrity house, an integrity house facility. You want to talk about experiencing racism? You have a drug war going on right now that's so affected my neighborhood. In fact, the design of my neighborhood, my city, and so many cities in America was a racist design because they redlined communities like this. They piled all of the low-income housing here as opposed to spreading it all throughout the state, suburban towns taking low-income housing, which would have been better for everybody. The air we're breathing here is more toxic than just a few towns over, because when they want to put all those things, where do they find it? They put the incinerator here in Newark. They put the highways through Newark. So you're air quality. So when you say experience racism, a lot of people go right to overt in your face, someone calling you the end word. What I'm telling you in America is to understand more fully. And you see this racial reckoning happening is everything that we're seeing from access to healthcare, environmental justice, the different access to drug treatment versus prison. I mean the people, I've sat with some of the fellows here in the integrity house and you go around the circle and guys have been arrested dozens of times before they ever had the access to the treatment that they needed. because their addiction, the disease was being treated like a criminal act. And every time they would get arrested, they would be put back on the street worse often than when they went in, because now they can't get a job, can't support their family, can't get a license, business license can't. And so we are so deeply affected by racism in America, that to say that you're not is then you're being willfully ignorant to the injustices that are around you.",
