  {
    "episode_id": 77710,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77710.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "I want to answer that for a second, but I just want to go back to what you're saying. I actually don't believe it starts at the top. I don't think this election coming up is a referendum on one guy in one office. I think this election is a referendum on us. I think it starts with us. I think any change in the world, you can't change it unless you're gonna be about it, you know? And I mean this very seriously, like, come on, Corey, powerful people, powerful places, no. Look at the history of this country, from the, you know, from the, from Stonewall, to Seneca Falls, to civil rights move, I can go through the people make the movements. And so we have to take more responsibility. Remember, King, when he wrote those famous letters from Birmingham Jail, he wasn't calling out the white supremacist governors. He said explicitly, I'm not, this is not about the KKK. This is not about the white citizens council. It's about moderates, white moderates, he called out specifically, you were doing nothing. And so the question's always gotta be about what are we doing to be the change we wanna see in this world? I was taught as a little kid, those 10, two letter words. If it is to be, it is up to me. If it is to be, it is up to me. And so this is one of those moments where I always say to people, you do not have to attend every argument you're invited to. Listen, I don't need to waste my time getting into an argument with somebody who's dead set against me. When I do need to spend more time with all those good people who agree with me, but don't feel like it's important enough to get up and vote. It would be, we wouldn't know Donald Trump right now. Let's just use black people for a second. If black folks in the state of Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, three states we lost by just 77,000 votes. If black folks had voted the same rate in 2016 as they had voted in 2012, It would be President Hillary Clinton right now. And I'm not putting it all on black people. There's a lot of people that didn't turn out in that election. And so what I'm just saying is we are powerful. All of us are powerful. And there's a Stanford researcher who measures things like this. They found ways to measure, if I'm walking down my own block here, and I bend over, pick up a piece of tracks. This researcher says, one good act, it flows in multiple degrees from you. If it's witnessed by others, it actually changes, it makes it more likely that they will do a good act. If you spend, all the people on the Instagram right now said, you know what? Every day, there is 102 days until the election in November. And once a day, I'm just going to put the words vote and a link to where you can get registered. You could change the behavior of enough people around you that you could actually change the course of human events. So what I'm saying is I agree with you about Donald Trump. But we give him too much energy, too much focus, too much emotion. I'm exhausted by the guy, frankly. What I'd rather be doing is sharing that emotion with people to get them focused on the fact that they have the power because in a democracy, The power of the people is always greater than the people in power if we use our power.",
