  {
    "episode_id": 77710,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77710.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "I felt some kind of way. But let me just tell you something, man. And let's say this is men first. Let's deal with this sort of gender. You and I both said boneheaded stuff about women, about what we have. because we've grown up in this patriarchy man, and we are saturated with that stuff. Our language, we've had to learn and grow, right? Doesn't mean we're bad people, but I've had my life, I've had a number of women have to set me straight. And so I'm not, I don't, I want to give people like Biden and others. I want to call them out when they're doing things that are offensive. That's what a friend would do. Biden is a friend. But I also want to leave them space to grow and to evolve. We all should be offered pathways to redemption because I've needed it. And so I didn't like what he said, but you know what I did like? Something Donald Trump would never do. I apologize. I don't think that Donald Trump has ever said those two words. But more than that, this is what makes Joe Biden to me, because I've had the privilege of spending time with the guy. I just like, especially at this moment where we need racial healing, to think that there might be a white man who's president, that Joe Biden might be president. And he might be one of those people that say, when he messes up, I'm not perfect. But America, this is a teaching moment for me, and maybe it can be for you too. I said this wrong. This is what I learned. We have got to be able to evolve as a country, because where we are right now, all of us have got to do better. And so when I see him showing that kind of masculinity, not the kind of says, I'm never wrong. I never apologize. It falls into a defensive crouch when somebody accuses you of something. No, he has this grace about him where he knows he ain't perfect. He knows he said something wrong. And by the way, it often happens when we're trying to make a joke. You and I both been there, when you try to be funny, and then you end up trampling on somebody's lawn. And so I just want you to know that this is not the last thing that Joe Biden's going to say that offends people. I am not done saying things that offend people, where I have to still learn. We've just got to make sure we have somebody that's willing to own up to their mistake. to look the American public in the eye, especially, what I don't like about this is when somebody does something racist, I don't like it when only black people are standing up and saying it's wrong. When somebody does something sexist, there's only women standing up. We need some allies out there, some straight people to defend the attacks on trans Americans. We need some white folks like Joe Biden that's going to stand up and call out racism. We need some men that are going to call out the assaults on women's reproductive rights because that's when we're all going to start to evolve. And so I have a lot of confidence in Biden's presidency because I've had enough conversations to know them that he has a level of grace and dignity and a humbleness that we need in the White House now more than ever.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Joe Biden demonstrated grace by acknowledging his mistakes publicly.",
        "confidence": 0.97
